@page "/five-minutes"

<PageTitle>Five minutes · Linq2Dashboard</PageTitle>

<h1>Try it in five minutes</h1>

<p>
    A dashboard over a hand-written list of orders, in a fresh Blazor app, with nothing to download. The whole dashboard is written
    in the page's markup: each facet and metric is defined where it is shown. Every step is a copy-paste. When it runs, replace the
    list with your own rows and keep the rest.
</p>

<h2>1. A new app and the packages</h2>

<p>A Blazor Web App with server interactivity, <code>Linq2Dashboard.Blazor</code>, which brings the <code>Linq2Dashboard</code> engine with it as a dependency, and QuickGrid for the rows. The commands are the same on Windows, macOS and Linux.</p>

<pre><code>@CreateAppExample</code></pre>

<h2>2. The rows</h2>

<p>Add <code>Order.cs</code> at the project root. A record and a dozen rows are enough to see every facet do something. Note the order without a country: null is a value and shows up as one.</p>

<pre><code>@RowsExample</code></pre>

<h2>3. The usings</h2>

<p>Append three lines to <code>Components/_Imports.razor</code> so every page sees the engine, the components and the grid.</p>

<pre><code>@ImportsExample</code></pre>

<h2>4. The page</h2>

<p>
    Replace the contents of <code>Components/Pages/Home.razor</code>. The line after the route is the one people forget: without
    <code>@@rendermode InteractiveServer</code> the page renders once and clicks do nothing.
</p>

<pre><code>@PageExample</code></pre>

<p>
    <code>Items</code> hands the view the rows, and the view builds the dashboard itself. Each component inside defines what it
    shows: a facet component with <code>For</code> defines that facet, with options such as <code>Buckets</code> and
    <code>Presets</code> as parameters, and a <code>Metric</code> with <code>Count</code> or <code>Sum</code> defines that metric.
    What is not shown on the page, here the order of the rows, goes in <code>Build</code>, which takes the same builder as
    <code>Dashboard.Create</code>.
</p>

<p>
    The rows are QuickGrid's, Microsoft's grid for Blazor, and could be any other grid. <code>Context="dash"</code> names
    the view's context in the markup, and <code>dash.Items</code> is one <code>IQueryable&lt;Order&gt;</code> per state:
    its reference changes after every click, so the grid re-queries then and never in between, and it pages, sorts and
    counts by itself over a list that is counted and indexable. The library renders no rows of its own.
</p>

<p>
    The template's <code>App.razor</code> already links <code>Shop.styles.css</code>, the app's scoped-CSS bundle. The components' styles
    are bundled into it, so there is no stylesheet or script to add, and nothing to register in <code>Program.cs</code>.
</p>

<h2>5. Run it</h2>

<pre><code>dotnet run</code></pre>

<p>
    Open the address it prints. Click <em>SE</em> under Country: the other countries keep their counts, Status and Amount narrow to
    Swedish orders, the tiles and the table follow, and the URL gains <code>?Country=SE</code>. Click the null value to see the order
    with no country. Remove the chip under the tiles to clear it. Reload the page: the selection comes back from the URL.
</p>

<h2>What to change next</h2>

<ul>
    <li><strong>Your data.</strong> Load your rows into a field, for example in <code>OnInitializedAsync</code>, and give the view that field: <code>Items="orders"</code>. The view builds again whenever the list reference changes, so never create the list in the markup. A build costs about 3 ms at 1 000 rows and 10 ms at 10 000, so building on every visit is fine for a user's own rows.</li>
    <li><strong>A large or shared dataset.</strong> When every user sees the same rows, or there are more than about 100 000, build once and cache: <a href="cached-dashboard">cache a shared dashboard</a> turns this app into that.</li>
    <li><strong>More facets and metrics.</strong> <code>ValueFacet</code> also takes <code>Top</code>, <code>Searchable</code>, <code>Label</code> and <code>Multiple</code> for a collection; <code>TextFacet</code> takes a <code>Match</code> function; <code>Metric</code> takes <code>Average</code>, <code>Distinct</code> or a <code>Formula</code> over other metrics. The <a href="usage">usage</a> guide lists every parameter and the rules: each facet is defined in one place, and a changed value such as <code>Top</code> rebuilds the dashboard.</li>
    <li><strong>Layout and styling.</strong> Every component takes <code>Class</code> and every colour and size is a <code>--l2d-*</code> custom property.</li>
    <li><strong>A larger starting point.</strong> The <a href="https://github.com/joadan/Linq2Dashboard/tree/master/samples/Linq2Dashboard.Sample" target="_blank" rel="noopener">sample project</a> in the repository has a page like this one over one country's orders, and a shared dashboard over 200 000 generated rows with scopes and a custom formatter.</li>
</ul>
